f Colorado  requires  225 words a minute and when I went to  Congress I read I wrote at 260 words a  minute by the time I got there the world  speed champion writes at 280 words a  minute so I mean it's a great feel it is  great and she asks you if you have to  slow them down in Nuremberg when I you  know when I do a longer a full  presentation I tell about how the court  reporters work Nuremberg we had half  shorthand reporters like I was with a  pen and a reporter's notebook half steno  type reporters and we could never nor in  Congress can we ever interrupt Ted  Kennedy anybody else who's speaking  especially a Ted Kennedy who never m's a  sentence and I reported him over a  period of three years but you just have  to sometimes we had congressmen who  loved to reel off a sentence as did Jim  Wright in Texas in Spanish and then we  wrote sounds and I went to the  congressman later and asked him to  translate for me or spell those Spanish  words in Spanish if that's what he  wanted to see in the congressional  record which nobody else could read but  in the courtroom you can if you get  behind or if you have a witness that is  not intelligible and you can't  understand it but I just don't ever  remember having to stop anybody now in  the nürnberg the interpreters are the  ones who had the problems because we  reporters had to wait for the  interpreters to translate the the words  and I don't know German but I understand  that in German the verb comes at the end  of the sentence  and so the interpreter had to wait until  the end of the sentence to get the verb  and the current reporter waited for the  interpreter and then he telescope that  sentence as fast as he possibly could  because the next sentence is already  being spoken but at any rate if a  reporter is qualified they can handle it  yes two questions  worthy were there other reporters who  translate or were they only transcribed  in English for the Nuremberg trials and  when did you decide to write your book  and tell us about that I'm going to  answer your second question first Tony  thank you very much for it this  reporting these trials caused me so many  problems I was so horrified by it that  when as soon as I got back to this  country I started having nightmares and  I was always escaping through a tunnel  for some reason with four or five small  children I was escaping from a  concentration camp and therefore it  might had been Tara seeing the  children's camp in a tunnel  and I was trying to keep these children  quiet because the Nazi guard was  marching back and forth along the barbed  wire fence with his bayonetted rifle I  didn't want the guard to hear the  children I kept looking for the light at  the end of that tunnel this went on for  three years and it was pure torture for  me and I was so horrified that I could  not speak about it I have empathy with  these survivors and I don't know whether  we have  I was in this room but I've spoken to I  think most of them in the Jewish  community here in Denver but I was so  horrified I never talked about it I  never talked about it I was in the  unofficial reporter in the Denver  District Court within eight years after  I got back from Nuremberg and I didn't  talk about it no one talked about it and  I just shoved it all in the back of my  mind and thought nothing about it until  1987 when in Denver we had a high school  teacher in a high school in Aurora who  referred to the Holocaust to her senior  students as the whole of Hawks and the  she claimed that she was trying to to  make her students do critical thinking  that at any rate I I was just livid and  I decided to get my material and  photographs and in my full presentation  I do a PowerPoint I couldn't do it here  tonight because of time but I have 38  captured German film and I got all of  that out which I had stored in boxes for  40 years and I put together a lecture  which I started giving a 1989 all over  the countries thousands and thousands of  students who were my target audiences  and then in 1995 the Steven Spielberg  Foundation interviewed me here in Denver  and I was beginning to get  confrontations by Holocaust deniers  claiming that it never happened and in  1999 I was speaking at the Long Beach  Hilton hotel to a good-sized audience  the LA Times gave me huge publicity in  advance  the day before and as a result the  Hilton Hotel received calls from quote  historians asking where and when in the  hotel I would be speaking and not  whether they could attend the hotel told  them it was a private professional  Association not open to the public they  demanded that they be allowed to go in  and the hotel called me the night before  I flew him to LA and asked me to  register under an assumed name they  would put on additional security  I flew in registered under another name  and I spoke on Saturday afternoon to  this large group my presentation is  about an hour and a half long including  the slides and at the end when people  were asking me questions suddenly the  the doors to the right of me flew open  an armed security guard with a gun on  his yep came up to me on the podium and  said quietly I have to take you off the  podium right now I knew something was  happening and I went right with him  behind the podium out the door into an  elevator there and down to the basement  for protection so at that time I decided  it's time I write this book and recently  a doctor asked me how I felt  doctors have received the book extremely  well asked me how I felt now that I have  the book finished and I said I have  found the light at the end of that  tunnel  [Applause]  they'll exist if so where are they  stored there in archives in Maryland I  say okay my notes of the Nuremberg  trials I said okay they're all in  archives and I want to tell you that the  court reporters transcript of the  doctor's trial is eleven thousand five  hundred thirty eight pages long in  archives and the staff the attorneys and  staff at